5 704 M
(\342\\200\\224 how they are to be examined, honored, rebuked, and set apart. Verses 21 and 22 sit ) s
5 691 M
(inside that block. "Do not lay hands on anyone hastily, nor share in other people's sins. Keep ) s
5 678 M
(yourself pure." The laying on of hands here is ordination. Paul is warning Timothy that if he ) s
5 665 M
(commissions a man who is not ready or not qualified, Timothy becomes responsible for the sins ) s
5 652 M
(and failures that follow. That is how a person "shares in the sins of others" in this passage. ) s
5 639 M
(Keeping yourself pure, in this context, means not contaminating yourself by a hasty or political ) s
5 626 M
(appointment.) s
5 600 M
(The qualifications themselves are strict enough to show why haste is dangerous. In 1 Timothy 3, ) s
5 587 M
(a bishop must be blameless, the husband of one wife, temperate, sober-minded, hospitable, able ) s
5 574 M
(to teach, not given to wine, not violent, not greedy, gentle, not quarrelsome, not covetous, ) s
5 561 M
(ruling his own house well. Titus 1 repeats the same kind of list for elders: blameless, not ) s
5 548 M
(self-willed, not quick-tempered, not given to wine, not violent, not greedy, hospitable, a lover of ) s
5 535 M
(good, sober-minded, just, holy, self-controlled, holding fast the faithful word. You cannot ) s
5 522 M
(evaluate a person against that list in five minutes. It takes a trajectory of observed life and ) s
5 509 M
(proven fruit.) s
5 483 M
(So Paul is telling Timothy: do not be pressured into ordaining someone because people like ) s
5 470 M
(him, or because he has money or standing, or because you want to fill a slot. Do not get pulled ) s
5 457 M
(into the politics of the thing. Examine the life. Look at the fruit. Wait until the man has actually ) s
5 444 M
(proven himself.) s
5 418 M
(That same caution applies to baptism. You cannot form a Christian in two or three weeks, much ) s
5 405 M
(less an Adventist. When candidates are rushed into the water before they have grown in ) s
5 392 M
(knowledge and surrender, the church inherits the problems. The pattern in Scripture is ) s
5 379 M
(gestation before birth, and real care after birth. Evangelism that neglects either of those stages ) s
5 366 M
(fills the church with people who are not ready and leaves new believers to fend for themselves. ) s
5 353 M
(The same principle that governs ordaining an elder governs baptizing a new member: do not be ) s
5 340 M
(hasty, and do not take on the sins that your haste creates.) s
5 314 M
(There is one further angle in the same family of texts. Romans 1:32 says of certain people that, ) s
5 301 M
(knowing the righteous judgment of God, they "not only do the same but also approve of those ) s
5 288 M
(who practice them." So sharing in the sins of others is not only done by ordaining the wrong ) s
5 275 M
(man or baptizing the wrong candidate. It is also done by approving and condoning others in ) s
5 262 M
(their sin. God holds the one who approves accountable along with the one who practices.) s
5 236 M
(What does it mean to heap coals of fire on an enemy's head in Proverbs 25:21-22?) s
5 210 M
(Heaping coals of fire on an enemy's head is not a hidden way to punish him. It is a Hebrew ) s
5 197 M
(idiom for the burning shame and moral awakening that comes over someone when he is met ) s
5 184 M
(with unexpected good instead of the evil he has been doing.) s
5 158 M
(Proverbs 25:21-22 says, "If your enemy is hungry, give him bread to eat; and if he is thirsty, give ) s
5 145 M
(him water to drink: for so you will heap coals of fire upon his head, and the Lord will reward ) s
5 132 M
(you." The instruction is straightforward. Do not repay evil with evil. Repay evil with good. Feed ) s
5 119 M
(the one who opposes you. Meet his need. Serve him. The "coals of fire" are what that kindness ) s
5 106 M
(produces in him, not a secret revenge dressed up as love. It is not passive aggression. It is not ) s
5 93 M
(covert vengeance. It is the piercing conviction that settles on a man when his hostility is ) s
5 80 M
(answered with mercy.) s
5 54 M
(First Samuel 24 shows the pattern in action. Saul with three thousand men was hunting David. ) s
5 41 M
(Saul went into a cave where David was hiding. David had every opportunity to kill him. ) s
5 28 M
(Instead David cut off the border of Saul's garment, let him leave unharmed, and then called out ) s
5 15 M
(to him publicly and showed him the evidence of the mercy he had just received. Saul's ) s
5 2 M
(response, in verses 16 and 17, is exactly the shame-and-awakening the proverb describes: "Is ) s
_R
S
