(Not every Israelite in the northern tribes lost their identity. A good portion intermarried with ) s
5 379 M
(the Assyrians and the surrounding pagan nations \342\\200\\224 that is the origin of the Samaritans ) s
5 366 M
(\342\\200\\224 but a remnant in each tribe remained faithful and kept the line. Josiah's reformation ) s
5 353 M
(shows it clearly: when he reached into the territory of the northern tribes, there were people ) s
5 340 M
(there who returned to the Lord.) s
5 314 M
(Levi is the easiest case, because the tribe of Levi was tied to the sanctuary service in Judah. ) s
5 301 M
(Many Levites returned with the post-exilic community to Jerusalem, so Elizabeth's Levitical ) s
5 288 M
(descent in the New Testament is not a surprise. Anna from the tribe of Asher is the harder case, ) s
5 275 M
(but the same principle applies: some remained, kept records, and preserved their tribal identity ) s
5 262 M
(through the captivity and after. By the New Testament period, tribal identity also does not ) s
5 249 M
(necessarily imply pure blood. Paul calls himself a Benjamite while living in Gentile territory. ) s
