5 691 M
(She captured the message in Testimonies to Ministers pages 91-92: the 1888 message presented ) s
5 678 M
(justification through faith in the Surety, Christ as the Surety, inviting people to receive the ) s
5 665 M
(righteousness of Christ \342\\200\\224 a justification that is made manifest in obedience to all the ) s
5 652 M
(commandments of God. And in Review and Herald, June 4, 1895, she wrote: "Righteousness ) s
5 639 M
(within is testified by righteousness without. The righteousness by which we are justified is ) s
5 626 M
(imputed. The righteousness by which we are sanctified is imparted. The first is our title to ) s
5 613 M
(heaven. The second is our fitness for heaven." You do not go to heaven with only one. The 1888 ) s
5 600 M
(message held both together. Rejecting it did not simply delay a revival; it delayed the third ) s
5 587 M
(angel's message in its fullness and with it the latter rain.) s
5 561 M
(What is the meaning of Revelation 9?) s
5 535 M
(Revelation 9 should be read as part of the historicist unfolding of the trumpets, not as a chapter ) s
5 522 M
(pushed wholesale into the future. The chapter contains the fifth and sixth trumpets, and the ) s
5 509 M
(speakers insist that the trumpets, like the churches and the seals, move through history step by ) s
5 496 M
(step from John's time to the end.) s
5 470 M
(They do not claim every detail of Revelation 9 is settled beyond dispute. They explicitly say ) s
5 457 M
(there are variations among Adventist interpreters on particular trumpet fulfillments. But they ) s
5 444 M
(treat one point as non-negotiable: the trumpets are not to be read as entirely future. In their ) s
5 431 M
(reasoning, Revelation 10 stands inside the sixth trumpet as a parenthesis, and Revelation 10 ) s
5 418 M
(applies to the Millerite movement and 1844. Because that section reaches into the sixth trumpet, ) s
5 405 M
(the sixth trumpet cannot belong only to the future. And because Revelation 11 presents the ) s
5 392 M
(seventh trumpet as the final takeover of the kingdoms of this world by the Father and Christ, ) s
5 379 M
(the sequence is still moving toward its climax.) s
5 353 M
(So the meaning of Revelation 9, in the way they answer it here, is not a full symbol-by-symbol ) s
5 340 M
(decoding of locusts and horsemen. It is that the fifth and sixth trumpets belong to the historic ) s
5 327 M
(course of redemptive history, that their fulfillment stretches through past events rather than ) s
5 314 M
(waiting entirely ahead, and that interpreters should stay with that historicist framework even ) s
5 301 M
(where they differ on some particulars.) s
5 275 M
(Is the "one hour" in Revelation 17:12 a literal time prophecy?) s
5 249 M
(No. The "one hour" in Revelation 17:12 is treated here as a short but indefinite period, not as a ) s
5 236 M
(literal prophetic time calculation.) s
5 210 M
(The first reason is the immediate context. Revelation 17:10 says the seventh king continues only ) s
5 197 M
("a short time," and verse 12 describes the ten kings receiving authority with the beast for "one ) s
5 184 M
(hour." The speakers treat those expressions as parallel. In other words, "one hour" is John's way ) s
5 171 M
(of describing brevity, not of supplying a calculable prophetic unit.) s
5 145 M
(The second reason is how Revelation itself uses the expression. In Revelation 18 Babylon's ) s
5 132 M
(judgment and destruction come in "one hour," but the point there is suddenness and rapid ) s
5 119 M
(collapse, not a literal sixty-minute destruction. They make the same point from Revelation 14:7: ) s
5 106 M
("the hour of his judgment has come" does not mean the judgment lasts only sixty minutes.) s
5 80 M
(The third reason is John's broader vocabulary. In the Gospel of John, Jesus says, "My hour has ) s
5 67 M
(not yet come," "the hour is coming," and later, "the hour has come" for the Son of Man to be ) s
5 54 M
(glorified. In those passages hour means an appointed time, season, or decisive moment. The ) s
5 41 M
(speakers say Revelation uses the term the same way here.) s
5 15 M
(So the ten kings receive authority with the beast for a brief, divinely noted period whose exact ) s
5 2 M
(limits are not specified in the text. The emphasis is on short duration, not on a literal time ) s
