(prophecy to be mathematically computed.) s
5 678 M
(Does the sixth commandment forbid self-defense?) s
5 652 M
(The sixth commandment forbids willful, violent, intentional taking of life. The speakers do not ) s
5 639 M
(flatten every case of death into the same category. They say Scripture itself distinguishes ) s
5 626 M
(between premeditated murder, accidental killing, vengeance, and cases where a death happens ) s
5 613 M
(without lying in wait.) s
5 587 M
(That is why they turn to Exodus 21 and Numbers 35. Exodus 21 says the person who strikes ) s
5 574 M
(another so that he dies is to be put to death, but it also makes room for a case where the person ) s
5 561 M
(did not lie in wait and may flee to the appointed place of refuge. Numbers 35 develops that ) s
5 548 M
(distinction further. If the death was not driven by enmity or premeditation, the congregation ) s
5 535 M
(had to investigate the case and the person could remain in the city of refuge rather than being ) s
5 522 M
(treated as a murderer. If it was premeditated murder, the person was delivered up. In their ) s
5 509 M
(answer, that distinction is the key.) s
5 483 M
(So they do not give a hard absolutist statement that every act of self-defense is automatically ) s
5 470 M
(sinful, and they do not give blanket approval either. They say a sudden life-or-death defense in ) s
5 457 M
(which someone is harmed or even killed may fall under the category of a non-premeditated act ) s
5 444 M
(rather than murder. At the same time, they openly admit they do not find a direct biblical ) s
5 431 M
(treatment of self-defense itself.) s
5 405 M
(They also add a pastoral caution. A believer should not be eager to take justice into his own ) s
5 392 M
(hands, because vengeance belongs to God, not to us. They point to Jesus' refusal to defend ) s
5 379 M
(Himself and raise the question whether we trust the Lord to deliver us without taking ) s
5 366 M
(vengeance ourselves. So the answer they give is restrained: premeditation and vengeance are ) s
5 353 M
(condemned; Scripture allows distinctions where intent and circumstance matter; and ) s
5 340 M
(self-defense remains a serious, complex question that should not be turned into a license for ) s
5 327 M
(revenge.) s
5 301 M
