5 288 M
(Romans 8:26-27 and John 14-16?) s
5 262 M
(The variation between "he" and "it" is a grammar issue, not a theology issue. In Greek, the word ) s
5 249 M
(for spirit \342\\200\\224 pneuma \342\\200\\224 is grammatically neuter, and the article that goes with ) s
5 236 M
(it is the neuter article. A literal translation can render that as "it." But when the context makes ) s
5 223 M
(plain that the subject is the Holy Spirit and the Holy Spirit is a person, translators shift to "he" to ) s
5 210 M
(reflect the referent rather than the grammatical gender. That is why the same translator can use ) s
5 197 M
(both within a few verses. Nothing in the Greek requires reading the Holy Spirit as an ) s
5 184 M
(impersonal force.) s
5 158 M
(The personhood itself is settled elsewhere. The Holy Spirit is the third person of the Godhead. ) s
5 145 M
(Ellen White wrote in Bible Training School, March 1, 1906, "There are three living persons of the ) s
5 132 M
(heavenly trio; in the name of these three," naming Father, Son, and Holy Ghost \342\\200\\224 and ) s
5 119 M
(that statement is from her directly, not a compilation. So three persons is the plain teaching, ) s
5 106 M
(even where the Greek article happens to be neuter.) s
5 80 M
(Beyond that, restraint is right. Ellen White also warns in Acts of the Apostles, page 52, that the ) s
5 67 M
(nature of the Holy Spirit is a mystery men cannot explain because the Lord has not revealed it; ) s
5 54 M
(bringing together passages to construct a human theory on something God has left unrevealed ) s
5 41 M
(will not strengthen the church. Silence is golden on the how. It is also worth noting that the ) s
5 28 M
(metaphors used for the Spirit \342\\200\\224 oil, rain, fire, dove, outpouring in the latter rain ) s
5 15 M
(\342\\200\\224 describe function, not substance. The Spirit is not literally a liquid being poured in; ) s
5 2 M
(those images picture what He does. And the Holy Spirit is not simply the divine nature of ) s
