(Christ transferred down to earth, because Christ's divinity and humanity are not separable in ) s
5 691 M
(that way. The Holy Spirit is His own person.) s
5 665 M
(If heaven has no sin or sadness, how will we remember loved ones who did not make it?) s
5 639 M
(The premise needs one correction before the answer. It is not quite right to say heaven has ) s
5 626 M
(never had sin or sadness. Sin originated in heaven, in a perfect place. And sadness is not sin ) s
5 613 M
(\342\\200\\224 the angels stopped their singing and laid down their harps during Christ's passion, ) s
5 600 M
(and God Himself sorrows when people reject Him. What is true is that heaven now has no sin, ) s
5 587 M
(because the devil has been cast out, and in the new earth there will be no more pain.) s
5 561 M
(On the memory itself, start with the placement of Revelation 21:4. Revelation 20 ends with the ) s
5 548 M
(wicked cast into the lake of fire. Revelation 21:1 is the climax of that scene: new heavens, new ) s
5 535 M
(earth, the old passed away. Then verse 2 begins a new cycle that loops back before the ) s
5 522 M
(destruction \342\\200\\224 the New Jerusalem descending after the millennium, before the wicked ) s
5 509 M
(are destroyed. Inside that earlier window comes verse 4: "And God will wipe away every tear ) s
5 496 M
(from their eyes; and there shall be no more death, neither sorrow, nor crying, neither shall there ) s
5 483 M
(be any more pain." Every verb in the surrounding passage is future from that point, and death ) s
5 470 M
(is still present because the wicked have not yet been destroyed. So the tears God wipes away ) s
5 457 M
(are real tears, shed by the saints inside the city over those outside the city. There will be grief. ) s
5 444 M
(God does not pretend otherwise.) s
5 418 M
(After that, the memory is not preserved as ongoing torment. Isaiah 65:17 is plain: "Behold, I ) s
5 405 M
(create new heavens and a new earth: and the former shall not be remembered, nor come into ) s
