5 275 M
(relying on foreknowledge, and how does this relate to the Godhead and the ministry of angels?) s
5 249 M
(Christ does not need to leave heaven and become locally present on earth in order to know ) s
5 236 M
(what is happening here. God knows everything from eternity, and Christ shares that divine ) s
5 223 M
(knowledge. At the same time, heaven does not operate in a flat, undifferentiated way. God ) s
5 210 M
(works through an ordered administration.) s
5 184 M
(That is why Revelation 1 is the starting point. The revelation is given by the Father to Jesus ) s
5 171 M
(Christ, then passed along through an angel to John, then to the churches, and from there to the ) s
5 158 M
(world. The Holy Spirit belongs in that line as Christ's representative on earth, because ) s
5 145 M
(Revelation repeatedly says, "Hear what the Spirit says to the churches." The point is not that ) s
5 132 M
(Christ is ignorant unless someone informs Him. The point is that heaven works through ) s
5 119 M
(delegated responsibility and active communication.) s
5 93 M
(Foreknowledge is not predetermination. God knew from eternity what choices would be made, ) s
5 80 M
(but His knowing did not force those choices. He does not determine the acts and then simply ) s
5 67 M
(watch them happen. He knows them because nothing is hidden from Him.) s
5 41 M
(Psalm 139 is used to make that distinction. The psalm is not treated as teaching that God is a ) s
5 28 M
(substance diffused through everything. It is treated as teaching that God knows everything. He ) s
5 15 M
(knows your sitting down and rising up, your thoughts afar off, and your words before you ) s
5 2 M
(speak them. So when the psalm says there is nowhere to flee from His presence, the point is that ) s
