(`Nephesh` and `ruach` are not the same word, but they are not describing two conscious parts of ) s
5 392 M
(a human being either.) s
5 366 M
(`Ruach` is the breath of life, the life-giving power from God that animates the body. That is why ) s
5 353 M
(Job 33:4 and James 2:26 are brought in. The spirit or breath is what gives life to the body. When ) s
5 340 M
(that breath is absent, the body is dead.) s
5 314 M
(`Nephesh` is the living person that results when the dust body is animated by that breath. ) s
5 301 M
(Genesis 2:7 is treated as the composition text: dust plus breath equals a living soul, that is, a ) s
5 288 M
(living being or person. So man does not have a soul as a detachable conscious entity. Man is a ) s
5 275 M
(soul.) s
5 249 M
(Ecclesiastes 12:7 gives the reversal of Genesis 2:7. The dust returns to the earth, the breath ) s
5 236 M
(returns to God who gave it, and the living person no longer exists as a functioning whole. That ) s
5 223 M
(is death. So the two texts are not fighting each other. One explains how a human being becomes ) s
5 210 M
(alive, and the other explains what happens when that life-process is undone.) s
5 184 M
(Scripture also uses `nephesh` in a broader way. It can mean life, person, self, or living being. ) s
5 171 M
(Abram acquired `nephesh` in Genesis 12:5, meaning people. The blood is called the `nephesh` in ) s
5 158 M
(Deuteronomy 12:23 in the sense of life. In the parable of the rich fool, speaking to his "soul" is ) s
5 145 M
(simply speaking to himself. The point is that the word does not automatically mean an ) s
5 132 M
(immortal conscious entity living inside the body.) s
5 106 M
(So when the spirit returns to God in Ecclesiastes 12:7, what returns is not a conscious person ) s
5 93 M
(traveling to heaven. It is the life that came from God. The conscious person was the composite ) s
5 80 M
(whole produced by body and breath together.) s
