(characters for the ultimate crunch time ahead.) s
5 522 M
(If the sun stood still for a whole day in Joshua 10:13, would the weekly cycle have been thrown ) s
5 509 M
(off, making the next Sabbath fall a day late?) s
5 483 M
(The weekly cycle has not been broken since creation week. That is not negotiable. The question ) s
5 470 M
(is how to account for Joshua's extra day without disrupting the count \342\\200\\224 and the ) s
5 457 M
(answer lies in how God defines a day.) s
5 431 M
(Ellen White in Testimonies to Ministers page 135 writes that God means "the day of 24 hours ) s
5 418 M
(which he has marked off by the rising and setting of the sun." On the day of Joshua's battle the ) s
5 405 M
(sun did not go down and the moon did not move. No sunrise opened it; no sunset closed it. By ) s
5 392 M
(God's own definition, that interval was not a day. It falls outside the week's count the same way ) s
5 379 M
(a fraction of time that has no rising and setting simply does not register as a day. The weekly ) s
5 366 M
(cycle was not interrupted; it ran continuously alongside a period that did not qualify as a day at ) s
5 353 M
(all.) s
5 327 M
(Ellen White in Signs of the Times, March 20, 1879, confirms the continuity: "The first week in ) s
5 314 M
(which God performed the work of creation... was just like every other week. The great God in ) s
5 301 M
(his days of creation and day of rest measured off the first cycle as a sample for successive weeks ) s
5 288 M
(till the close of time. The weekly cycle of seven literal days... has been preserved and brought ) s
5 275 M
(down through Bible history." She does not name Joshua as an exception. The cycle held.) s
5 249 M
(Ellen White adds in Patriarchs and Prophets page 509 that this miracle "testifies that the ) s
5 236 M
(creation is under the control of the creator" \342\\200\\224 it was a demonstration of divine ) s
5 223 M
(authority, not a disruption of divine order.) s
5 197 M
(A practical point on doubt: if Sunday-keepers maintain that the day they keep is the same day ) s
5 184 M
(Christ rose \342\\200\\224 trusting the continuity of the weekly cycle across the centuries \342\\200\\224 ) s
5 171 M
(then the Sabbath must be equally intact. You cannot hold the resurrection-day continuous and ) s
5 158 M
(the creation-Sabbath scrambled by the same count.) s
5 132 M
